{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"rubbery-adamantine-panda","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Observation\nIn a sandboxed local `make verify` (worktree almondy-drop-acl-step, Nov 19 18:23 to Nov 20 01:14 PST, load average around 40), pytest-timeout (300 s, thread method) killed workers in `tests/unit/metrics/test_git_metrics.py::test_oracle_cap_change_events_compares_unsampled_adjacent_revisions` (inside `git_metrics._git` from `_py_blobs_at` / `module_size_trend`) and in 3 tests of `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_plan_policy_dogfood.py` (inside the `_cli` subprocess helper). The same change passed CI Verified (Gerrit 3475).\n\nMeasured 2026-11-20 on this host:\n- test_oracle_cap_change_events alone: 416 s at load ~30-40 (passes only under a 900 s budget); 90 s at lower load. A counting git shim records 606 git starts per run: 182 from the test's own setup (60 x `add`, `commit`, `rev-parse`) and about 410 from the code under test (240 `ls-tree`, 170 `cat-file`, for 60 revisions).\n- Deterministic reproduction: a PATH git shim that sleeps 0.5 s before every git start (606 x 0.5 s = 303 s) makes the test hit `+++ Timeout +++` every run.\n- test_in_progress_to_open_resets_phase_and_requires_a_planning_review alone: 57 s at load ~25 (14.7 s setup) with 265 git starts plus several `python -m rebar.cli` subprocesses.\nNot a hang: the processes were alive and advancing (sampled child list showed successive `git add -A` / `git commit` for qualified 0..12 within 60 s).\n\n## Hypotheses (unproven)\n- The budget failure is driven by start count times per-start latency; the 300 s budget was sized from CI timings where a git start costs tens of milliseconds.\n- `git_metrics` walks history with one `ls-tree`/`cat-file` per revision and blob, which is also a production cost on long histories.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Under the 0.5 s-per-start shim, test_oracle_cap_change_events completes inside the 300 s budget (for example by building its fixture history with one `git fast-import` and batching blob reads in `git_metrics` through `cat-file --batch`), with the shim run recorded here.\n- [ ] The plan-policy dogfood tests are measured under the same shim and either fit the budget or carry a justified `@pytest.mark.timeout(N)` per the pyproject policy.","id":"10c6-8881-c05e-4209","parent_id":"","priority":3,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Git-spawn-heavy tests exceed the 300 s per-test budget when each git start costs 0.5 s or more"},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1795180663983115001,"uuid":"53a55893-79a2-49b5-8602-f64c2c95726a"}